WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.970
- Okay, so be honest.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.510
I, and, and when I say, be honest,

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.470
I mean, lying is totally okay.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.416
How did I do with your song?

00:00:07.416 --> 00:00:08.249
(laughing)

00:00:08.249 --> 00:00:09.177
Did you hear it?

00:00:09.177 --> 00:00:10.130
- Awesome.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.010
I noticed that you had to change the key,

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.470
It was in a girl key.

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:15.270
- I know I'm a first soprano, man.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.080
I always have to raise
everything 'cause I can't go low,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.810
I can go high.
(laughing)

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.070
- Yeah.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.510
God bless you for it,

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:23.710
but yeah, it was cool.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.000
Thank you for doing that.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.120
- Oh my gosh, no, I'm such,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:27.132
we're all such big fans.

00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:30.290
So where were you in life
when you wrote It's My Life

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.019
and what does it mean to you?

00:00:32.019 --> 00:00:34.330
- For me, it was Frankie,

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:35.660
like there's a lyric and it says,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.630
like Frankie said, I did it my way.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.690
That was from New
Jersey's most famous son,

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.120
Frank Sinatra

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.810
and I had just come home from doing a film

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.840
in Malta and Rome

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.500
called U-571,

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.450
it was a World War II picture
with Matthew McConaughey

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:54.113
and we'd come home and we're
making the Crush record

00:00:54.113 --> 00:00:57.030
and, and we got together to write that

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.870
and I had to fight very
hard for that lyric.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:01.345
And, and Richie said to me,

00:01:01.345 --> 00:01:03.840
"What is Frankie and what does it matter?:

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.980
And I says, "Well,
because much like Frank,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.870
I'm gonna make movies when
I want, records when I want

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.180
and this is how I'm feeling."

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.980
And I won the argument simply

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.420
because I had to sing
the song every night,

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.910
but, and that's what it stood for for me.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.130
But the magic was is guys like Anthony

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:20.353
and people around the world,

00:01:20.353 --> 00:01:22.260
they said, no, Frankie is me,

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.930
Frankie's my best friend,
Frankie's my brother

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.680
and that's the magic of a great song

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.350
is people make it their own.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.730
- Yeah. I love the message,

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:30.740
yeah that you can get.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:31.730
That's the most interesting thing

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:33.560
I think performing shows

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.780
and meeting fans
afterwards and everything,

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.680
everybody has their own
kind of take on a song

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.420
that you had a completely
different idea about

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:39.253
and why you wrote it.

00:01:39.253 --> 00:01:41.550
And I think that's what's
so magical about music.

